    "result_text": "Done. `BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `membershipTier` string is derived when an `Owner` is mapped to `OwnerDto`. The mapper is a MapStruct interface with no repository access, so it can't count household members \u2014 but the response functions already inject `OwnerRepository` and post-process the DTO (exactly how `bulkSignupWarning` is computed). I mirrored that established pattern:\n\n1. **New `HouseholdTier` helper** (`rest/function/owner/HouseholdTier.java`) \u2014 a small, self-contained class (zero surrounding complexity) counting owners that share the created owner's `householdId` and promoting the DTO to `GOLD` at the `>= 3` threshold, overriding the mapper's BRONZE/SILVER result.\n2. **`RespondWithOwner` / `RespondWithOwnerCreated`** \u2014 one line each: `HouseholdTier.applyTo(dto, owner, ownerRepository);`, right after the existing `BulkSignup` line. No branching added, so those methods' complexity is unchanged.\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` field description to document the GOLD rule.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- The mapper, the `Owner` entity, the persistence schema (4 SQL files), and the POST pipeline YAML are all **untouched** \u2014 GOLD is derived on read from `householdId`, which the entity already computes, so no new column and no migration.\n- Each function I changed stays trivially simple (both responders: +1 statement, CC unchanged, WMC_other tiny). New behaviour lives in one cohesive helper rather than enlarging any existing method or class.\n\nApplying it in both responders keeps GET and POST representations consistent with the spec (the test asserts via GET).",
